      <h1>Six wheels, solar power—and a smarter cold-weather check</h1>
      <p class="s04-deck">For owners of six-wheel motorhomes and dually setups, a tire pressure monitoring system can make it easier to keep an eye on all six tires from the driver's seat.</p>
      <a class="s04-button" href={affiliate} target="_blank" rel="sponsored noopener noreferrer">Check Price ↗</a>
      <p class="s04-disclaimer">Affiliate link. We may earn a commission at no extra cost to you. This is a research-based product overview, not a hands-on test.</p>
    </div>
    <a class="s04-hero-image" href={affiliate} target="_blank" rel="sponsored noopener noreferrer" aria-label="View the Grundig S04 six-wheel TPMS">
      <img src={image} alt="Grundig S04 solar six-wheel tire pressure monitoring system" fetchpriority="high" />
    </a>
  </header>

  <p class="s04-intro">A cold snap is a good reminder to check tire pressure before a trip. Ambient temperature changes can affect readings, and a long RV or dually setup can make manual checks feel like a chore. The Grundig S04 is designed to show pressure information for six wheels on one anti-glare LCD receiver, with solar charging and USB-C as additional charging options.</p>

  <div class="s04-facts">
    <div><span>Designed for</span><strong>Six-wheel setups</strong></div>
    <div><span>Listed pressure range</span><strong>29–217 PSI*</strong></div>
    <div><span>Charging</span><strong>Solar + USB-C</strong></div>
    <div><span>Advertised price band</span><strong>$109–$160*</strong></div>
  </div>
  <p class="s04-footnote">*Specifications and pricing can vary by selected configuration and may change. Confirm the exact variant and current listing before ordering.</p>

  <h2>Why the S04 stands out</h2>
  <p>The S04 is aimed at drivers who want a dedicated six-wheel display rather than a generic monitor that requires more setup to cover their wheel layout. The receiver includes a solar panel and a 500 mAh battery, with Type-C charging available when sunlight is limited. External sensors are described as IP67-rated, and their batteries are user-replaceable.</p>

  <div class="s04-feature-grid">
    <section><span class="s04-number">01</span><h3>One view for six wheels</h3><p>A single receiver is designed to display pressure readings for a six-wheel configuration, useful for dual-rear-wheel motorhomes and dually vehicles.</p></section>
    <section><span class="s04-number">02</span><h3>Two ways to recharge</h3><p>Solar charging can help top up the monitor, while USB-C provides another option when the unit has had limited exposure to light.</p></section>
    <section><span class="s04-number">03</span><h3>Replaceable sensor batteries</h3><p>The manufacturer describes sensor batteries as user-replaceable, with an approximate one-year life under stated use conditions. Actual life varies.</p></section>
  </div>

  <h2>The cold-weather setup step not to skip</h2>
  <p>Do not assume the factory alarm threshold is correct for your rig. The default high-pressure alarm may need adjustment for the tire and vehicle setup, but the right threshold is not simply the maximum PSI printed on a tire sidewall. Check the vehicle placard, tire manufacturer's guidance, and the load and inflation requirements for your specific tires. Set alerts in accordance with the manufacturer's instructions and never lower tire pressure just to silence an alarm.</p>
  <p>Before driving in colder weather, check pressures when the tires are cold, inspect the tires for visible damage, and confirm that each sensor is paired with the correct wheel position. Follow the vehicle and tire makers' instructions for cold inflation pressures. A TPMS is an additional monitoring aid—not a substitute for routine inspections or a calibrated pressure gauge.</p>

  <aside class="s04-note"><strong>Before your first trip:</strong> Confirm the correct alarm settings, verify all six sensor positions, check that the receiver is charged, and test that each sensor is reporting before you leave.</aside>

  <h2>Who should consider the S04?</h2>
  <p>It may be worth a closer look if you drive a dual-rear-wheel Class C motorhome, a Super C, a six-wheel dually setup, or another compatible six-wheel vehicle and want a dedicated in-cab display. Owners of longer rigs should check the manufacturer's guidance on signal range and whether a booster is recommended for their particular vehicle.</p>
  <p>It is less straightforward if your setup has more than six monitored wheels, uses a different sensor type than the selected package, or requires a configuration the product does not support. Read the exact variant details carefully: internal-sensor and external-sensor packages are not interchangeable assumptions.</p>

  <div class="s04-product-card">
    <img src={image} alt="Grundig S04 six-wheel solar TPMS receiver and sensors" loading="lazy" />
    <div><p class="s04-kicker">THE PRODUCT AT A GLANCE</p><h2>Grundig S04 Solar TPMS</h2><p>A six-wheel monitoring option with solar and USB-C charging. Check the listing for your preferred sensor configuration, pressure range, included accessories, and current price.</p><a class="s04-button" href={affiliate} target="_blank" rel="sponsored noopener noreferrer">Check Price at Grundig Auto ↗</a><p class="s04-small">Prices, availability, and included components can change on the advertiser's site.</p></div>
  </div>

  <h2>Our Buy Better Finds take</h2>
  <p>The S04's appeal is its focused six-wheel format and flexible charging, rather than a claim that a monitor can eliminate tire maintenance. For the right RV or dually owner, a clear in-cab display may make routine monitoring easier. The important buying step is matching the exact sensor package and alarm setup to your vehicle and tire requirements.</p>
  <p>We have not personally tested this system. Our overview is based on the manufacturer's published product information, so confirm the latest specifications and setup instructions before purchase.</p>
